WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:20.840
If you were to ask someone to name a famous
nurse, there’s an extremely high chance

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.269
they’d respond with the name of one 19th
Century woman.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:27.090
Florence Nightingale is almost the archetype
of nurses.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.769
A wealthy woman who gave it all up to devote
herself to caring for the sick, she’s still

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:36.550
pictured today as she was during her heyday
in the Crimean War, carrying a lamp from sickbed

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.970
to sickbed, tending to the wounded.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.980
But how many of us know the woman behind the
myth?

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:45.910
Born into privilege in the early 19th Century,
Florence Nightingale was a polymath genius

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:51.590
who spoke multiple languages and pioneered
concepts of statistical analysis still used

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:52.630
today.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:57.379
A shy, devout Christian, she was a feminist
who thought women shouldn’t vote; a celebrity

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:02.410
who wished only to be forgotten; and a nurse
who oversaw a hospital with one of the highest

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.629
death rates in modern history.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:10.800
A complex woman full of contradictions, this
is the life of Florence Nightingale.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:18.460
A Woman’s Place
What words spring to mind when you hear the

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:19.720
name Florence Nightingale?

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.990
We’re guessing it’s something along the
lines of ‘nurse’, ‘angel’, and possibly

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:23.990
‘lamp’.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.560
What probably don’t spring to mind are words
like ‘borderline genius’.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.790
Born on May 12, 1820 to English parents in
the city of Florence –no prizes for guessing

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:40.189
where she got her name from – Florence Nightingale
had the sort of brain that only comes along

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:41.560
once in a generation.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.950
Her parents were super-connected, super-wealthy
English socialites who valued education, even

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.460
for their two daughters.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.360
This was sort of a big deal at the time, when
being born with two X chromosomes was the

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:58.500
quickest shortcut to a life of knitting, wearing
corsets, and fainting whenever someone mentioned

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.500
long division.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.969
Luckily for baby Florence, her father had
always dreamed of passing on his own Cambridge

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:08.870
education to his children, and he’d be damned
if he was going to let those children being

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.600
female from stopping him.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.570
There’s actually some evidence Papa Nightingale
had expected Florence to be a boy and refused

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.940
to accept reality.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.230
He encouraged the villagers on his estates
to refer to the young girl by the male title

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.230
“squire”.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:27.130
Regardless, Florence’s early life was spent
in a blitz of learning that would leave an

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.500
honor student’s head spinning.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.430
She mastered French, German, Greek, Italian
and Latin.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.950
She memorized works of philosophy and would
debate them with her father.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.850
She also got super into math, to the extent
that she begged her parents to let her go

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.540
and study the subject at university.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.250
Her mother told her no.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.520
It was not long after that Florence had her
vision.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.140
We need to be careful with the word “vision”,
because it conjures, well, visions of people

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.540
like Joan of Arc communicating directly with
God.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.510
Florence’s vision wasn’t like that.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:03.060
It was more a feeling than anything, a certainty
that God had given her a silent command.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.850
Still, the effect was dramatic.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:11.460
On February 5, 1837, the sixteen year old
Florence declared God had told her to end

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.060
suffering in the world.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.590
She interpreted this to mean she should go
into nursing.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.150
This time, her mom and pop both told her no.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.510
In those days, nursing was considered barely
above prostitution.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:28.610
The idea that an upper-middle class girl would
go into the profession was so radical even

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.770
Florence’s liberal father refused to consider
it.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:36.310
But if mom and pop thought they could keep
Florence from her calling, they had another

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.310
thing coming.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:42.650
Over the next 13 years, Florence tried again
and again to change her parents’ minds.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:47.550
In 1844, Florence tried, and failed, to convince
her parents to send her for nurse training

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.700
in Salisbury.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:54.240
In 1849, she even ditched a long-term suitor,
Richard Monckton Milnes, so she could focus

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.180
on her non-existent career.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.310
Random aside: it later transpired that this
suitor was heavily into sadomasochism, so

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.200
maybe actually kinda dodged a bullet there?

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.010
Anyway, Florence was persistent.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:11.580
She kept working away at her parents all the
way from her teenage years, right into womanhood.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.350
By the time 1850 rolled around, she was 30
and her father was utterly worn down.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.810
That July, her parents gave her permission
to travel to Germany for a two week training

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.950
course at a nursing hospital.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:27.160
Perhaps they thought being up close with all
that disease would put her off nursing for

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.160
life.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.160
But, no.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:32.870
The next year, Florence returned for another
course, this one lasting three months.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.460
By 1853, Florence’s dream had been realized.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:40.230
Using her family connections, she got a job
at a hospital for “distressed gentlewomen”

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.940
on Harley Street in London.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.820
It wasn’t hugely taxing, but it was definitely
nursing.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.621
Finally, Florence Nightingale had made it!

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.540
But it would soon transpire that this was
just the warm up act.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.590
Florence Nightingale didn’t know it yet,
but she was about to get sucked into one of

00:04:53.590 --> 00:05:03.390
the bloodiest conflicts in European history.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.650
The Sick Man of Europe
In 1853, the European continent was haunted

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.250
by the specter of a sick man.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:11.410
The Ottoman Empire had once been one of the
finest empires history ever produced.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.840
At its height, it was an epicenter of science
and art capable of taking territory all the

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.240
way up into Austria.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.590
By 1853, though, the glory days were over.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:24.881
Hobbled by stagnation, secession, and wars
of independence, the Ottoman Empire was by

00:05:24.881 --> 00:05:30.100
now staggering on the fringes of geo-politics,
known as “the sick man of Europe”.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:34.650
As the invalid empire hacked away on its deathbed,
another empire sensed an opportunity.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:39.710
In the Russian capital, it was lost on absolutely
no-one that a dying Ottoman state meant a

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:42.520
chance to snatch territory from the empire’s
fringes.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.350
At the same time, in Paris and London, it
was lost on absolutely no-one that a territory-hungry

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:50.720
Russia posed a real threat to them all.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.340
In October 1853, all these different strands
finally came together.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:00.330
Under a pretext of defending Orthodox Christians
living in the sick empire, Russia made its

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.330
move.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:05.240
The Turks declared war, which pulled in Britain
and France and suddenly the area around the

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.110
Black Sea was alight with conflict.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:13.130
It was the start of the Crimean War, and it
would end somewhere in the region of a million

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.130
lives.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.440
We could easily spend the next 20 minutes
describing this complex, forgotten war, but

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.000
for our purposes today, there are two things
you need to understand about the Crimean War.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.480
The first is that this was the first war in
which the modern media was involved.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:29.500
The first war correspondent in history, William
Howard Russell, was there for the Times of

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.770
London, sending back real-time reports via
the newly-invented telegraph.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:38.130
The second is that military hospitals were
very different from what we’d expect now.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.320
Nursing soldiers during wartime wasn’t something
governments did.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:45.470
Since medieval times, it had usually been
religious orders like the Sisters of Mercy

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:46.860
who took care of the wounded.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.630
When the Crimean War erupted, though, the
British government was so against the idea

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:55.830
of sending women into a conflict zone that
they actually forbid any nurses from going.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.330
This meant the injured British troops were
receiving a level of care that was so basic,

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.110
you could program computers with it.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.160
Yep, that was a terrible coding pun, and no,
we’re not sorry.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.460
The upshot is that these two points overlapped
when William Howard Russell decided to write

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.860
about British military hospitals in Crimea,
and sent back reports so graphic they caused

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:14.100
outrage.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:18.500
Caught back-footed, the British government
quickly reversed its “no nurses on the frontlines”

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:19.500
policy.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:23.220
Suddenly, Secretary of State at War Sidney
Herbert found himself in dire need of a head

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.100
nurse to send to Crimea.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.940
Luckily, he had just the woman in mind.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:30.990
Many years earlier, in the 1840s, Herbert
and his wife Elizabeth had been visiting Rome

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:34.410
when they happened to bump into a vacationing
Florence Nightingale.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:38.360
The three hit it off, and remained close friends
when they returned to England.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:43.639
So it was, in 1854, that Herbert was able
to write to Nightingale and ask her to go

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:44.639
to Crimea.

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:45.639
Rather than saying “You want me to walk
unarmed into a slaughterhouse?

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:47.210
Gee, thanks,” Florence Nightingale jumped
at the chance.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:52.370
Put at the head of 38 volunteer nurses, the
37 year old left behind her hospital for gentlewomen

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:54.340
and got on a boat on October 21, 1854.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:59.800
It was meant to be a dream come true, a chance
to show her parents what she was really capable

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:00.800
of.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:06.200
Instead, Florence Nightingale would soon find
herself trapped at the center of a nightmare.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:14.419
“The Kingdom of Hell”
On November 5, 1854, Florence Nightingale

00:08:14.419 --> 00:08:19.540
and her corps of nurses arrived at the British
military hospital in Scutari – today a suburb

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:20.620
of Istanbul.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:24.560
What they found there would haunt them for
the rest of their lives.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.250
The hospital was built atop a sewer, which
had flooded long ago and no-one had bothered

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:30.250
to plug.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.700
When injured men walked to the toilets, they
had to pass barefoot through a layer of ankle

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.150
deep feces.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.550
There were rodents running amok.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.330
Men lying in filthy bedclothes that hadn’t
been changed for weeks on end.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:45.440
There was rotting meat lying around from where
patients were supposed to cook their own meals.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.820
People with unwashed, gangrenous wounds.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:53.460
It was a human cesspit, a place where pestilence
and disease roamed the hallways, trailing

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.120
death in their wake.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.310
The shock was so great that, for the first
few days, it was all Florence Nightingale

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:00.930
could do to keep functioning.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:05.220
The military brass made it clear after she
landed that they didn’t want her rocking

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.120
their stinky, disease-ridden boat.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.850
So she tried not to.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.000
Really, she did.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.440
This became tricky after November 10.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:16.080
On that day, the British wounded of two major
battles arrived in Scutari.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.980
The hospital, already in dire need of supplies,
effectively ran out.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.440
It was during this time, when she was surrounded
by squalor and misery, and the screams of

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:29.540
the dying, that Nightingale famously called
her new hospital “the Kingdom of Hell.”

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:33.890
But the experience gave her the kick up the
rear she needed to get moving.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.560
Remember how we said Florence Nightingale
was super into math as a teenager?

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:40.570
Well, she started crunching the numbers for
the hospital.

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:44.210
To her amazement, she discovered that war
injuries had killed around 4,000 people in

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:45.690
the hospital that year.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:47.660
Sickness, on the other hand, had killed 19,000.

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:48.660
This wasn’t a hospital.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.589
It was a charnel house.

00:09:51.589 --> 00:09:55.470
Soldiers were coming in with treatable injuries
and leaving in coffins.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:59.150
Faced with an army determined to keep doing
things the old way, Nightingale had no choice

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:03.190
but to start trying to save those soldiers
herself.

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:07.089
This is the part where we get to the legendary
image of Florence Nightingale, the nurse walking

00:10:07.089 --> 00:10:12.170
the wards after dark, armed only with her
lamp, offering succor to the dying.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:13.660
Certainly, this happened.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:17.010
Nightingale’s nighttime visits became a
fixture of the wounded men’s lives.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.810
But, really, this romanticized image is just
a sideshow.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:23.440
The important stuff was happening behind closed
doors.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:27.380
In meetings with army officers, Nightingale
insisted on basic standards of cleanliness

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:31.830
at Scutari, including regular bathing of patients,
and changing bandages on wounds.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:36.240
At the same time, she campaigned for the open
sewer flooding the lower floors to be repaired,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:40.610
something we’re having trouble believing
she actually needed to “campaign” for.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.670
Of course, cleanliness and closing sewers
doesn’t make good copy, so the newspapers

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:48.950
that wrote about “the lady with the lamp”
focused more on her bedside manner.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:52.850
There were the tales of Florence Nightingale
helping the wounded write letters home.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:56.940
Of the night time visits she made to keep
the men’s spirits up.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.290
Incidentally, the reason Nightingale was the
only one to visit the wards at night was because

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.500
the other nurses were forbidden to.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.600
Nightingale thought they would have sex with
the male patients, so banned everyone else

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.930
from roaming around after dark.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:11.010
Just a weird historical fact we couldn’t
fit in elsewhere.

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:15.240
Before long, the men at Scutari had grown
to love their “ministering angel”, and

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.440
the British public had grown to love her,
too.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:19.620
That’s right, Nightingale became a celebrity.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:24.980
The tales of her selfless good work were exactly
what Britain needed from a war that had so

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.910
far produced no heroes.

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:30.680
This reached its apex on February 24, 1855.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.400
That day, a London newspaper published an
engraving of Nightingale with her lamp.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.350
Overnight, this random nurse working in a
British military hospital became more than

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:39.350
just a woman.

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:41.020
She became a symbol.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:44.040
The days of Florence Nightingale, the human,
were over.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:12:10.959
The days of Florence Nightingale, the lady
with

00:12:10.959 --> 00:12:53.330
the lamp, had arrived.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:13:15.529
The Lady with 
the Lamp

00:13:15.529 --> 00:13:34.360
Today, the truth of Florence Nightingale has
almost been lost to the myths swirling around

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.310
her name; both good and bad.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:40.910
For example, you’ve maybe heard how Scutari
actually had a higher death rate under Nightingale,

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:44.029
whose reforms were more often than not fatal
for patients.

00:13:44.029 --> 00:13:47.450
This is a tale that’s cropped up before
on places as venerable as the BBC.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:50.430
It’s also likely complete bull poop.

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:54.710
Actual Nightingale scholars like Mark Bostridge
are adamant this is based on a misreading

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:58.790
of the data – sort of like how you might
look at the way WWII combat deaths spiked

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:04.000
after September, 1939, and conclude Winston
Churchill was the bad guy.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.019
Yes, deaths at Scutari jumped under Nightingale.

00:14:07.019 --> 00:14:11.340
But that’s because she arrived just as supplies
ran out and the hospital exceeded capacity.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:15.050
That’s not to say the lady with the lamp
was always a saint.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:18.769
The same time that Nightingale was at Scutari,
around 1855, a British commission looking

00:14:18.769 --> 00:14:21.910
into sanitation proposed similar hospital
reforms to hers.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:26.540
To this day, some think Nightingale effectively
stole their ideas and passed them off as her

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:27.540
own.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.500
Then there’s the issue of Mary Seacole.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:34.730
A half-Jamaican “doctress” who used herbal
remedies, Seacole traveled to the Crimea under

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:38.700
her own steam and set up a hospital on the
peninsula to treat sickened officers.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.350
She and Nightingale were aware of one another,
and didn’t like each other very much.

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:46.310
There’s some evidence Nightingale may have
refused to let Seacole join her nursing corps

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:47.820
due to her race.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:51.990
On the other hand, suggestions that Nightingale
took credit for Seacole’s innovations don’t

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.540
seem to have much grounding in historical
sources.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:58.269
Come 1855, Nightingale was a hero of the British
Empire.

00:14:58.269 --> 00:15:02.120
Back in London, a fund had been set up in
her name that would eventually raise £45,000,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:07.600
equivalent to a really, really big pile of
cash in today’s money.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:10.660
The nurse had also won respect for getting
her hands dirty.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:14.620
On a trip to Crimea itself, she’d been badly
sickened and nearly died.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:18.290
While the papers celebrated her recovery,
no-one knew she’d picked up brucellosis,

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.430
a recurring infection that would soon leave
her badly disabled.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:23.500
But that was all in the future.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:28.260
Until the war ended, all Florence Nightingale
wanted to focus on was caring for her patients.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:32.510
On March 30, 1856, end it finally did.

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:37.220
The Treaty of Paris was the end of the Crimean
War, a mass slaughter that had killed a million

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:39.170
but changed very little.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.890
Those million that had died, by the way?

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:44.060
Almost all of them were carried off by disease.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:48.149
After the war wrapped, Nightingale stayed
on in Scutari to help close the hospital,

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:49.620
before finally departing herself.

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:53.970
On August 7, 1856, she arrived back in Britain
to a hero’s welcome.

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:54.970
Seriously.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:59.540
People were treating her like the second coming
of Elvis… and Nightingale absolutely hated

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:00.540
it.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:04.519
As she saw it, the hysteria over the Lady
with the Lamp was just a way of distracting

00:16:04.519 --> 00:16:10.370
from the real issues: the reform the army
medical establishment desperately needed.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:14.579
In the end, she decided she’d just have
to push through these reforms herself.

00:16:14.579 --> 00:16:19.040
In Sickness and…

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:26.050
In the mid-19th Century, if you got sick,
there was only one explanation: miasma.

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:28.721
Miasma theory was one of those things you
sometimes hear about in history that sound

00:16:28.721 --> 00:16:33.290
so screwy you almost can’t believe people
actually thought it was real.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:36.220
Basically, people thought that diseases were
caused by bad smells.

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:38.720
The cholera you might contract from raw sewage?

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:42.430
That wasn’t because of germs, but because
of the smell of poop.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:46.910
Not long before the Crimean War, miasma theory
had become so widely accepted that the British

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:51.360
government actually approved a plan to drain
sewage into drinking water, because that would

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.450
be less harmful than letting it pong on the
surface.

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:55.450
Yuck.

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:59.630
By the time Florence Nightingale came along,
miasma wasn’t the only theory in town.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:03.730
Many medical professionals were sounding alarm
bells that maybe this new germ theory was

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.159
where it’s at.

00:17:05.159 --> 00:17:09.449
But miasma was still the theory British politicians
and army medical brass believed.

00:17:09.449 --> 00:17:15.159
And Florence Nightingale was about to explode
all that as effectively as a stack of dynamite.

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:19.679
After her return home, Nightingale devoted
all her energy and intellect to getting a

00:17:19.679 --> 00:17:24.289
Royal Commission established to look into
preventable deaths in the Crimean War.

00:17:24.289 --> 00:17:26.400
But the army stonewalled her.

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:31.940
So Nightingale teamed up with government statistician
William Barr to provide evidence that something

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.549
needed to be done.

00:17:33.549 --> 00:17:38.030
In 1856, statistical analysis was at the cutting
edge of modern science.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:43.650
All those lovely, easy-to-understand charts
you see on Nate Silver’s blogs didn’t

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:44.650
exist yet.

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:48.980
The fact they do is in part thanks to Nightingale
and Barr.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:52.669
For roughly a year, the two worked to crunch
the data by hand.

00:17:52.669 --> 00:17:56.600
They compared the records of the hospital
at Scutari to a London military hospital,

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.179
and then also a Manchester civilian hospital
as a control.

00:18:00.179 --> 00:18:05.019
As the excellent Bedside Rounds podcast details
– and, really, their episode on Florence

00:18:05.019 --> 00:18:11.760
Nightingale is a great resource for understanding
her statistical breakthroughs – miasma theory

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:15.720
suggested the hospital in Manchester should
be the worst of all.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:19.230
Manchester in 1856 was a choking, billowing
cauldron of stench.

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:22.980
It was everything miasma believers feared.

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:26.359
But Nightingale and Barr’s data showed something
very different.

00:18:26.359 --> 00:18:30.570
The death rates in the Manchester civilian
hospital were far below those in the London

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:31.700
military one.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:36.500
While the death rate in Scutari had initially
outpaced even the London hospital, after Nightingale’s

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:39.059
reforms, it had dropped into second place.

00:18:39.059 --> 00:18:43.570
A military hospital in the civilized British
capital had a higher death rate than a field

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:46.380
hospital on the edges of an active warzone.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:51.230
As Nightingale put it, sanitation in British
army hospitals was so bad, it was akin to

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:57.419
taking 1,100 young men out onto Salisbury
Plain each year and shooting them dead.

00:18:57.419 --> 00:19:01.169
When Nightingale and Barr presented their
findings, they did so visually, using what

00:19:01.169 --> 00:19:03.230
we’d now call a coxcomb graph.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:07.980
It’s said that this was likely one of the
first times in history data was presented

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:08.980
visually.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:10.230
That made it easier to understand.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:13.769
When the government saw her graphs, Nightingale
was vindicated.

00:19:13.769 --> 00:19:17.549
A major review of army medical standards was
undertaken with sweeping effect.

00:19:17.549 --> 00:19:21.950
The reforms the lady with the lamp had first
started all the way back in Scutari were implemented

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:26.480
across the board, including methods for cataloguing
disease and death rates that were still in

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:29.140
use well into the 20th Century.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:32.710
By the time the British Army intervened in
China’s bloody Taiping Rebellion in the

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:38.059
early 1860s, Florence Nightingale’s recommendations
had so taken root that the death rate for

00:19:38.059 --> 00:19:42.740
soldiers from disease was 90% lower than it
had been in the Crimean War.

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:45.159
For Nightingale, this was a major triumph.

00:19:45.159 --> 00:19:49.559
The last half decade of her life had been
consumed with forcing army medical culture

00:19:49.559 --> 00:19:53.820
to change, and now here she was directing
those changes.

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:56.690
But there would be no time for celebration.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:20:01.309
In 1857, Nightingale suffered her first collapse
due to the brucellosis infection she’d picked

00:20:01.309 --> 00:20:02.409
up in Crimea.

00:20:02.409 --> 00:20:05.090
In no time at all, the disease had emaciated
her.

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:11.109
She lost her hair, lost weight, became effectively
bedridden, often suffering pain so acute she

00:20:11.109 --> 00:20:12.210
was unable to work.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:22.740
For the rest of her life, Britain’s most
famous nurse would live in agony.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:24.619
The Body Declines
From a physical standpoint, that was pretty

00:20:24.619 --> 00:20:26.100
much it for Nightingale.

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:30.059
After coming down with her illness, she retreated
to bed and never really left.

00:20:30.059 --> 00:20:36.070
But while she’d never again go as far away
as Crimea, the polymath nurse wasn’t done

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:37.070
yet.

00:20:37.070 --> 00:20:38.309
She was just getting started.

00:20:38.309 --> 00:20:40.820
Confined to her bed, Nightingale started writing
letters.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:41.820
Hundreds of them.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:42.820
Thousands of them.

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:47.179
Campaigning letters going out to all corners
of the country, outlining the need for major

00:20:47.179 --> 00:20:48.500
medical reforms.

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:49.539
And that’s not all she did.

00:20:49.539 --> 00:20:53.580
Remember how people in Britain raised £45,000
for her while she was in Scutari?

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:58.929
Well, Nightingale took that money, and used
it to found a secular nursing school at St

00:20:58.929 --> 00:21:00.369
Thomas’s Hospital, London.

00:21:00.369 --> 00:21:02.879
The Nightingale School wasn’t the first
nursing school in Britain.

00:21:02.879 --> 00:21:05.989
But it was the first that fulfilled three
strict criteria.

00:21:05.989 --> 00:21:07.249
One: it was secular.

00:21:07.249 --> 00:21:10.519
Two: it developed its program in line with
scientific advances.

00:21:10.519 --> 00:21:13.859
And, three: it treated nursing as a career.

00:21:13.859 --> 00:21:17.210
No longer would nursing be something for nuns
and the lower classes.

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:19.869
Nightingale wanted to make nursing a legitimate
profession.

00:21:19.869 --> 00:21:23.419
You can see she succeeded just by looking
around you today.

00:21:23.419 --> 00:21:27.570
Nowadays, those who see nursing as synonymous
with prostitution are strictly stupid, horny

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:30.460
men with a tragic thing for uniforms.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:32.570
But this is where it all started.

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:36.649
Nightingale’s school was the first to give
nurses good pay, sick leave, annual vacation.

00:21:36.649 --> 00:21:40.619
Perhaps most importantly, though, the school
encouraged its nurses to travel.

00:21:40.619 --> 00:21:44.279
To set up schools in other countries and pass
the message on.

00:21:44.279 --> 00:21:47.970
Within a few years, Nightingale nurses had
fanned out across the British Isles.

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:51.820
A few years after that, they were establishing
schools in America.

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:55.789
A few years after that, they were even setting
up hospitals in Japan.

00:21:55.789 --> 00:21:59.590
As they went, the Nightingale nurses took
some core tenets with them.

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:03.070
Nurses should be separate to doctors, rather
than merely subordinate.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:05.919
They should enforce cleanliness and the washing
of wounds.

00:22:05.919 --> 00:22:08.509
And healthcare should be available for everyone.

00:22:08.509 --> 00:22:12.700
If you live in a nation with universal healthcare,
it may well be thanks in part to Florence

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:13.760
Nightingale.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:17.740
From her bed, the great medical reformer tirelessly
advocated for equal treatment of the poor

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:19.190
by nurses and doctors.

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:23.070
It’s this belief that eventually evolved
into Britain’s modern NHS.

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:26.970
Of course, there was more to Nightingale’s
last years than just medicine.

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:30.010
She became the first female fellow of the
Royal Statistical Society.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:33.059
She advocated for minority rights across the
British Empire.

00:22:33.059 --> 00:22:37.929
She also wrote one of the key texts of 19th
Century feminism: Casandra, a book detailing

00:22:37.929 --> 00:22:42.479
how intelligent women are often ignored by
less-intelligent men, which, wow, unfortunately

00:22:42.479 --> 00:22:44.200
still seems pretty relevant.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:48.470
But perhaps the most interesting thing about
Florence Nightingale’s later life is how

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:49.979
private it was.

00:22:49.979 --> 00:22:54.769
Famously prickly, the now-elderly lady with
the lamp hated receiving visitors, hated having

00:22:54.769 --> 00:22:57.500
her picture taken, hated being reminded of
her fame.

00:22:57.500 --> 00:23:01.940
Towards the end of her life, she declared
“I only want to be forgotten,” and changed

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:05.299
her will to stipulate she shouldn’t have
a public funeral.

00:23:05.299 --> 00:23:09.249
Finally, on August 13, 1910, the 90-year old
Nightingale breathed her last.

00:23:09.249 --> 00:23:13.080
For the British public, it was like a symbol
of the nation had been lost.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:17.970
Despite her pleas for a private funeral, people
lined the road all the way to Nightingale’s

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:19.259
final resting place.

00:23:19.259 --> 00:23:22.769
Government ministers made speeches at a special
service at St Paul’s cathedral.

00:23:22.769 --> 00:23:26.850
Although Nightingale had specified that she
wanted to be buried in an anonymous, unmarked

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:31.419
grave, she was finally put to rest beneath
a headstone bearing her name at St Margaret’s

00:23:31.419 --> 00:23:32.419
Church.

00:23:32.419 --> 00:23:35.240
Today, the legend of Florence Nightingale
is still going strong.

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:38.190
She’s endured as a symbol in a way few ever
will.

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:41.910
Every year, nurses still carry lighted lamps
into Westminster Abbey in London, to mark

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:43.799
the passing of the lady with the lamp.

00:23:43.799 --> 00:23:45.249
It’s a touching ritual.

00:23:45.249 --> 00:23:46.249
Poignant.

00:23:46.249 --> 00:23:50.019
It’s also something Miss Nightingale probably
would have hated.

00:23:50.019 --> 00:23:51.849
Florence Nightingale didn’t want to be remembered.

00:23:51.849 --> 00:23:55.409
She didn’t want to have lamplight processions
and YouTube videos about her life.

00:23:55.409 --> 00:23:56.779
She simply wanted to vanish.

00:23:56.779 --> 00:23:58.159
To be anonymous.

00:23:58.159 --> 00:24:03.229
But, sometimes, someone lives a life so great
that forgetting them simply isn’t an option.

00:24:03.229 --> 00:24:06.789
When Florence Nightingale was born, women
were considered incapable of understanding

00:24:06.789 --> 00:24:08.890
concepts like statistics.

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:12.389
Nursing was considered unsuitable for middle
class girls.

00:24:12.389 --> 00:24:15.080
Being healthy meant not sniffing smelly air.

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:18.859
The fact that Florence Nightingale was able
to not just challenge all of these assumptions,

00:24:18.859 --> 00:24:22.359
but help overturn them explains why she’s
remembered today.

00:24:22.359 --> 00:24:26.190
Born into a world with a glass ceiling so
low it was practically a cage, Nightingale

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:30.249
managed to smash through and, in doing so,
change the world.

00:24:30.249 --> 00:24:33.909
She may have been a living legend who wished
to be forgotten, but she was also something

00:24:33.909 --> 00:24:34.909
else.

00:24:34.909 --> 00:24:39.370
A symbol for both the wounded and the dying,
and those who wished to tend for them.

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:53.999
She was, if nothing else, the Lady with

00:24:53.999 --> 00:25:00.440
the Lamp.

